# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The main risk with using delegatecall for the rewardsProxy is that it gives the rewardsProxy contract full access to the state and logic of the PrincipalToken contract. This could allow a malicious rewardsProxy contract to drain funds, manipulate state like balances or rates, pause the contract, etc.
Some major impacts this could have:
• Drain all deposited assets from the vault by redeeming the IBT and sending funds to the attacker
• Manipulate rates to artificially inflate yields, misleading users
• Freeze deposits/redemptions by pausing the contract
• Transfer ownership/privileges by changing access control
The severity here is very high given the privilege escalation. A malicious rewardsProxy could essentially take full control. Using a simple call instead of delegatecall would limit the blast radius if the rewardsProxy contract itself is compromised. Additional safeguards like rate limiters could help, but avoiding delegatecall is the most robust mitigation.


## Proof of Concept
